latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Scan the input grid:\n   a. Create a set of all unique colors present in the grid, excluding black (0).\n   b. For each unique color, store its positions in the grid.\n\n2. Determine the output orientation:\n   a. If there are 2 or fewer unique colors, set orientation to horizontal.\n   b. If there are 3 or more unique colors, set orientation to vertical.\n\n3. Determine the color order:\n   a. Sort the unique colors based on their first appearance in the grid, scanning from top-left to bottom-right.\n\n4. Create the output grid:\n   a. If orientation is horizontal:\n      - Set height to 3\n      - Set width to number of unique colors * 3\n   b. If orientation is vertical:\n      - Set height to number of unique colors * 3\n      - Set width to 3\n   c. Initialize the grid with all black (0) cells.\n\n5. Generate and place color patterns:\n   For each color in the sorted order:\n   a. Create the 3x3 pattern:\n      [0, color, color]\n      [color, color, 0]\n      [0, color, color]\n   b. If orientation is horizontal:\n      - Place the pattern in columns (i*3) to (i*3+2), where i is the index of the color in the sorted list.\n   c. If orientation is vertical:\n      - Place the pattern in rows (i*3) to (i*3+2), where i is the index of the color in the sorted list.\n\n6. Create and return a new ColoredGrid with the generated output grid.\n\nThis plan addresses the key points from our reflection:\n- It considers all unique colors, not just those in a single line.\n- It correctly generates the 3x3 pattern for each color.\n- It determines the orientation based on the number of unique colors.\n- It orders the colors based on their first appearance in the input grid, which should match the observed behavior in the examples.\n\nThis approach should work for all the given examples and test cases, as it's based on the patterns we identified across all of them. It's flexible enough to handle different numbers of colors and different input configurations, while still producing the expected output format and orientation."
